      "notes": "<h2>Prepositions of Direction and Movement</h2>\n<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING MOVEMENT PREPOSITIONS</h3>\n<p>Prepositions of direction and movement describe how someone or something moves from one place to another.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Into / Out of:</b> Entering or leaving an enclosed space (<i>She walked into the classroom; He stepped out of the car</i>).</li>\n<li><b>Onto / Off:</b> Moving to or from a surface (<i>The cat jumped onto the roof; The cup fell off the table</i>).</li>\n<li><b>Across / Through:</b> Across a 2D surface (<i>swim across the river</i>) vs through a 3D volume (<i>walk through the forest</i>).</li>\n<li><b>Towards / Away from:</b> In the direction of or moving further from an object (<i>She ran towards the bus</i>).</li>\n</ul>",
